(Bug #61 - a pad width was read with a 32-bit parse whose Err was dropped,
 so a width past i32::MAX built the same spec as no width at all: no
 padding, no diagnostic. The width that survived the parse was then written
 one write(2) per character, at about 265 KB/s. Widths are counts with no
 ceiling (LANGUAGE.md:3107-3108), so both halves are fixed here: any width
 the compiler can hold reaches the printer, and the printer emits it a page
 at a time.

 The last line is 4100 characters wide - past the 4096-character page the
 padding is written in - so it fails on a chunk loop that drops or repeats
 its final partial page. The width past i32::MAX itself is pinned in
 src/codegen/tests.rs, where it can be asserted on the emitted instruction
 rather than by writing two billion spaces.)

a number called n is 255.
a number called owed is 0 subtract 42.

(Every padded form, at a width small enough to read.)
Print "[{n:8}]".
Print "[{n:08}]".
Print "[{owed:8}]".
Print "[{owed:08}]".
Print "[{n:8x}]".
Print "[{n:08x}]".
Print "[{n:8X}]".
Print "[{n:12b}]".
Print "[{n:012b}]".
Print "[{n:8o}]".
Print "[{n:08o}]".

(A zero-padded hex field keeps its base however many leading zeros the
 width is written with.)
Print "[{n:04x}]".
Print "[{n:004x}]".

(Wider than one page of padding.)
Print "{n:4100}" without newline.
Print "|".
